page('The complete software stack','02 / Separate hardware enablement, control and packet forwarding',
 diagram(['Administrator / fleet platform|Browser · SSH · MQTT','Custom Python services / LuCI|Settings · telemetry · industrial data','OpenWrt service layer|procd · UCI · ubus · netifd · D-Bus · ModemManager','Linux kernel|Drivers · IP routing · bridges · netfilter · conntrack','Board peripherals + external modem|Ethernet · Wi-Fi · USB · eMMC · SIM / LTE'], 'Settings flow down the stack; operational state and events flow back up.',True)+
 p('<b>Control plane:</b> UCI stores policy; netifd configures interfaces and routes; ModemManager sets up the modem; firewall4 produces nftables rules. The Python application coordinates these components.')+
 p('<b>Data plane:</b> Linux moves ordinary client packets between interfaces using the installed routes and rules. Internet packets do not pass through the web server or ModemManager. Industrial proxies and VPN programs can process their own payload streams.')+
 p('<b>Hardware enablement:</b> firmware, device trees and drivers make peripherals usable before router policy exists. Linux cannot compensate for an unpowered modem, missing SIM, wrong device tree or incompatible driver.')+
 note('The Cortex-A35 application processors run Linux. A custom Cortex-M33 routing program is not required for this documented packet path. The cellular baseband runs its own firmware inside the Quectel module.'))

page('ST firmware and the boot sequence','03 / The software that makes Linux possible',
 diagram(['Boot ROM|inside STM32','TF-A BL2|DDR + loading','Secure payloads|BL31 + OP-TEE','U-Boot|OS selection','Linux|kernel + DTB'], 'Simplified STM32MP25 AArch64 handoff; secure firmware continues to provide runtime services.')+
 table(['Component','Responsibility','Developer impact'],[['Boot ROM','Read boot selection; load early firmware or enter programming mode.','Boot failure here is below Linux; inspect boot pins, power and programming path.'],['TF-A BL2','Early platform initialization and loading of later firmware.','DDR and platform compatibility belong to the BSP/firmware build.'],['TF-A BL31','Secure monitor and low-level runtime interfaces such as power management.','Resident after Linux starts; not an IP routing daemon.'],['OP-TEE / BL32','Trusted execution and ST platform secure services.','Can participate in protected resource management; not a substitute for Linux drivers.'],['FIP packaging','Bundle later firmware payloads for the platform boot chain.','Keep firmware pieces from a compatible BSP release.'],['U-Boot / BL33','Choose slot; load kernel and DTB; provide boot arguments.','A/B selection and fallback depend on saved environment and the project boot hook.'],['Linux + rootfs','Probe devices, mount root, start OpenWrt userspace.','A kernel that boots without the correct root UUID still cannot start services.']])+
 p('Use a serial boot log to separate ROM/firmware, U-Boot, kernel and userspace failures. A web outage alone cannot identify which stage failed.')+
 note('TF-A and OP-TEE being present does not prove authenticated secure boot is enforced. This guide does not assert the kit’s fuse or secure-boot provisioning state. Upstream: ST boot-chain overview and TF-A STM32 platform documentation [U1–U2].'))

