page('LTE connection: two parallel paths','06 / Modem control is separate from modem packet data',
 diagram(['netifd|WAN request','ModemManager|D-Bus API','MBIM control|cdc-wdm','Modem firmware|SIM + carrier'], 'Control path: configure APN, unlock/inspect SIM, register and establish a bearer.')+
 diagram(['LAN packet|Linux routing','WWAN netdev|cdc_mbim','USB host|packet transfer','Modem radio|LTE network'], 'Data path after setup: packets use the modem network interface.')+
 table(['Stage','Software action','If it fails'],[['1. Enumerate','USB core and drivers bind the Quectel functions.','Inspect power, cable, USB IDs and kernel logs before changing APN.'],['2. Discover','ModemManager groups ports into one modem and finds its SIM.','Check driver binding, daemon logs, SIM detection and PIN state.'],['3. Register','Modem firmware performs cellular registration under selected policy.','Check antenna, RSRP/RSRQ/SINR, operator, SIM entitlement and coverage.'],['4. Connect bearer','ModemManager requests APN/IP family and receives bearer IP settings.','Check APN, roaming and carrier restrictions.'],['5. Configure host','netifd integration applies address, route, MTU and DNS as appropriate.','A connected bearer alone does not prove host routing works.'],['6. Forward clients','Firewall/NAT and LAN DNS/DHCP permit client traffic.','Test separately from router-originated traffic.']])+
 p('The project records Telstra and APN <code>telstra.extranet</code>; this is a recorded profile, not a universal APN recommendation. A private APN can intentionally provide private-network reachability without public Internet.')+
 note('Discover modem IDs and tty numbering at runtime. The old names end0 and wwu1u1i8 are historical observations. See ModemManager WWAN/IP setup documentation [U4].'))

page('Follow one packet through the router','07 / DHCP, DNS, routing, NAT and the return path',
 diagram(['Laptop|192.168.10.50','LAN bridge|192.168.10.1','Forward filter|conntrack','WAN SNAT|LTE address','Carrier|destination'], 'Illustrative IPv4 routed/NAT flow; actual WAN address is supplied by the carrier.')+
 p('<b>Before the packet:</b> dnsmasq leases the client an address, gateway and DNS options. DNS resolves a hostname. The client sends an Ethernet frame to the router’s MAC because the destination is outside its subnet.')+
 p('<b>Outbound:</b> the Ethernet driver receives the frame. Linux identifies the IP destination, consults policy/routing tables, checks forwarding policy and connection state, then masquerades the source on the WAN egress. The USB WWAN driver delivers it to the modem.')+
 p('<b>Return:</b> conntrack associates the response with the original flow; reverse NAT restores the LAN destination, routing selects the LAN and the driver transmits to the client.')+
 table(['Traffic type','Kernel / userspace path','Practical distinction'],[['Client to Internet','Forward path + NAT','An outbound firewall forwarding rule is involved.'],['Client to web UI or SSH','Router input path','This is router administration, not a port forward.'],['Router telemetry to broker','Local output path','A client-forwarding data cutoff may still allow this.'],['Inbound to LAN service','DNAT + forward path','Requires carrier reachability and explicit forwarding policy.'],['LAN to another LAN device','May remain in switch/bridge','Routed WAN policy may never see it.'],['OpenVPN payload','Tunnel via userspace OpenVPN, then kernel','CPU work and MTU differ from ordinary forwarding.']])+
 note('Carrier-grade NAT (CGNAT) is upstream of this device. DDNS, a local DMZ rule and passthrough cannot remove it. Use a reachable carrier service or an outbound VPN for remote access.'))

